Four more callers, and the feature with two ends.

M164 found twenty undefined symbols and wrote down what nearly every one of
them was: a caller an earlier milestone left behind when it gated a
definition out. These are the rest of them, found by the first link that got
past WebGPU. Three are that same thing again. The fourth is not, and it is
the one worth reading.

INOTIFY AND LANDLOCK, IN THE SAME FUNCTION

content/app/content_main_runner_impl.cc calls
base::GetMaxNumberOfInotifyWatches() to pre-read
/proc/sys/fs/inotify/max_user_watches before the sandbox closes, and
sandbox::policy::ReportLandlockStatus() to ask whether Landlock is there.
The first call is guarded by nothing at all; base/BUILD.gn compiles the
inotify watcher under has_linux_kernel and ships file_path_watcher_stub.cc
otherwise. The second is guarded by IS_LINUX || IS_CHROMEOS - the family -
while sandbox/policy/BUILD.gn compiles landlock_util.cc under the condition
patch 0047 gave it. Landlock is a Linux Security Module, which is to say a
kernel interface. M144's line, twice in one function.

THE SECOND V4L2 FACTORY

M164 gated media/capture's V4L2 camera and fixed "the factory that names
it". There are two factories. video_capture_buffer_tracker_factory_impl.cc
is the other one:

  #elif BUILDFLAG(IS_LINUX)
    return std::make_unique<V4L2GpuMemoryBufferTracker>();

where media/capture/BUILD.gn has compiled video/linux under has_linux_kernel
since patch 0044. The arm at the end of that chain returns nullptr, which is
what every platform Chromium has no GPU buffer tracker for already gets.

A FEATURE HAS TWO ENDS, AND GATING THE MOJOM BREAKS BOTH

net::NetworkChangeNotifierPassive is compiled in net/BUILD.gn under
is_android || is_chromeos || (is_linux && has_linux_kernel), because it keeps
an AddressMapCacheLinux - the address map that rtnetlink fills in. Five
places in //services/network still named it: the mojom feature
network_change_notifier_in_browser, NetworkChangeManager::OnNetworkChanged in
the header AND the source, NetworkChangeNotifierPassiveFactory, and the
network_service.cc line that installs that factory.

What the feature MEANS is why five was not enough. The browser process
watches for network changes and PUSHES them to a sandboxed network service,
which runs a passive notifier because it cannot watch anything itself. On
Linux what the browser watches is rtnetlink. So there is a second end:
content/browser/network_service_client.cc is the pusher, and it calls
OnNetworkChanged from three places behind IS_ANDROID || IS_LINUX.

Gating the mojom feature is not like gating an implementation. It removes a
METHOD FROM AN INTERFACE, so every user on both sides of that interface stops
compiling at once - which is how the pusher was found, and only after the
receiver had been gated and built. An implementation gated out is found by a
link; an interface method gated out is found by a compile, in files nobody
was looking at.

This is the third time in this port that a feature has needed every site
before it was off - libsync in M159 took two directories, and
use_network_interface_change_listener in M160 took two enables in one
BUILD.gn. Both files edited here already carry M163's has_linux_kernel on
SOME of their blocks and not others, which is what made the remaining ones
easy to miss: a guard that is present thirty lines away reads as a guard that
is present.

A platform in the Linux family with no rtnetlink has nothing to push and no
passive notifier to push it into. net::NetworkChangeNotifier's own default is
what every platform outside that list already gets.

Chromium's tree still contains the word lean_os nowhere.

